Step 2: Identify actors
Actors are the decision-makers in the model.
Possible actors include:

offender;
victim;
witness;
police;
prosecutor;
defendant;
judge;
prison authority;
regulator;
firm;
peer group;
community;
platform;
criminal organization.

A model should not include every possible actor. Include actors only if their choices are central to the mechanism.

Step 4: Specify information
Ask what each actor knows when choosing.
Information may include:

detection probability;
sanction severity;
police presence;
evidence strength;
co-offender reliability;
institutional trust;
victim willingness to report;
type of offender;
strength of social ties;
platform rules;
legal consequences.






flowchart LR
    A[Actor] --&gt; B[Observed information]
    A --&gt; C[Unobserved information]
    C --&gt; D[Beliefs]
    B --&gt; E[Choice]
    D --&gt; E






If actors are uncertain, beliefs must be part of the model.

Step 5: Define payoffs
Payoffs represent what actors care about.
In criminology, payoffs can include:

money;
status;
revenge;
fear;
shame;
punishment;
time;
effort;
moral cost;
legal risk;
stigma;
trust;
safety;
future opportunity;
institutional legitimacy.

Do not assume payoffs are purely financial unless the model requires it.

Target suitability
A target is more suitable when it is valuable, visible, accessible, removable, and weakly protected.
For example, a bicycle locked in a hidden alley with a weak lock is more suitable than a bicycle locked in a crowded area with strong surveillance.
Target suitability depends on:

value;
portability;
visibility;
accessibility;
traceability;
guardianship;
resale possibilities.

Guardianship
Guardianship does not only mean police. It can include:

neighbours;
passers-by;
staff;
lighting;
locks;
alarms;
CCTV;
platform moderation;
transaction monitoring;
design of space.

Guardianship changes the expected risk and difficulty of offending.

Institutional response
Institutions affect violent crime through several channels:

emergency protection;
deterrence;
incapacitation;
support for victims;
conflict mediation;
legitimacy and trust;
weapon control;
treatment and rehabilitation;
hotspot or focused deterrence strategies.

The same intervention may have different effects depending on whether the violence is domestic, group-based, situational, or economically motivated.

True crime and recorded crime
A central measurement problem is the gap between crime that occurs and crime that appears in official data.





flowchart TD
    A[True crime incidence] --&gt; B{Is the event noticed?}
    B --&gt;|No| C[Invisible event]
    B --&gt;|Yes| D{Is it reported?}
    D --&gt;|No| E[Unreported crime]
    D --&gt;|Yes| F{Is it recorded by authorities?}
    F --&gt;|No| G[Reported but not recorded]
    F --&gt;|Yes| H[Recorded crime]
    H --&gt; I[Official crime statistics]






This does not make official statistics useless. It means they should be interpreted carefully.
Official statistics are especially useful when paired with:

victimization surveys;
self-report surveys;
administrative records;
qualitative evidence;
local institutional knowledge;
careful comparison over time.

Institutions and incentives
Criminology is also about institutions. Police, courts, prisons, probation systems, regulators, communities, and firms all respond to crime under constraints.
Institutions face their own incentives:

limited budgets;
imperfect information;
political pressure;
legal constraints;
legitimacy concerns;
discretion;
error;
unequal visibility across groups and places.

A good institutional analysis asks not only whether an intervention works, but also how it changes incentives and behaviour across the system.

Information
Information is often the most important part of criminological modelling.
Actors may be uncertain about:

detection risk;
sanction severity;
police presence;
victim reporting;
co-offender loyalty;
evidence strength;
court outcomes;
institutional response;
peer behaviour;
market conditions.

Incomplete information can change the result completely. It can create signaling, screening, reputation, mistrust, overreaction, or hidden behaviour.

Payoffs and constraints
Payoffs do not need to mean money. In criminology, payoffs may include:

material gain;
physical risk;
punishment;
shame;
status;
revenge;
addiction;
fear;
stigma;
legal cost;
moral cost;
future opportunity;
institutional legitimacy.

A good model should avoid pretending that all motivations are the same. But it should still be clear about what is being assumed.

Policing
Policing is not only enforcement. Police also produce information.
Police activity affects:

detection;
recorded crime;
perceived risk;
public trust;
community cooperation;
informal order;
visibility of certain groups;
legitimacy of law.

This makes policing central to both crime control and crime measurement.
A simple policing mechanism may look like:





flowchart LR
    A[Patrol or monitoring] --&gt; B[Perceived detection risk]
    B --&gt; C[Behavioural response]
    A --&gt; D[Actual detection]
    D --&gt; E[Recorded crime]
    A --&gt; F[Community experience]
    F --&gt; G[Trust and cooperation]






The same intervention can increase recorded crime by increasing detection, even if underlying offending falls.

Adverse selection
Adverse selection occurs when one side has private information before exchange.
Examples:

insurance buyers know more about their risk than insurers;
workers know more about ability than employers;
borrowers know more about default risk than lenders;
sellers know more about product quality than buyers.

Screening tries to design choices that reduce the problem.

Screening and criminology
Screening logic can also appear in criminology and institutions.
Examples:

police use behaviour or context to infer risk;
courts use plea decisions to infer case strength;
offenders use tests to screen co-offender reliability;
platforms design reporting systems to distinguish abuse from noise;
regulators design audits to induce compliance.

The same logic applies: private information creates selection problems.

Sender and receiver
The sender has information. The receiver has decision power.
For example:

an expert advises a policymaker;
a seller describes product quality;
a suspect gives an explanation;
a doctor recommends treatment;
a friend gives advice;
a manager reports project progress;
a lobbyist communicates with a regulator.

Cheap talk appears whenever one side knows something and the other side must decide how much to trust the message.

Cheap talk and deception
Cheap talk is useful for thinking about deception because messages can move beliefs without being costly or binding.
However, deception is not always simply “lying.” A sender may:

reveal fully;
pool with other types;
exaggerate;
understate;
send vague messages;
stay silent;
use ambiguity strategically.

The receiver’s interpretation depends on equilibrium beliefs.

Why signaling matters
Many social and economic actions are partly informative.
Examples:

education may signal ability;
prices may signal quality;
costly effort may signal commitment;
apologies may signal remorse;
investment may signal confidence;
violence may signal toughness;
compliance systems may signal responsibility;
credentials may signal competence.

The key question is whether the signal separates types or merely creates appearance.

Education signaling example
In an education signaling model:

workers know their own ability;
firms do not observe ability;
workers choose education level;
firms observe education and offer wages;
education is less costly for high-ability workers.






flowchart LR
    A[Worker ability type] --&gt; B[Education choice]
    B --&gt; C[Firm observes education]
    C --&gt; D[Firm updates belief]
    D --&gt; E[Wage offer]






Education may not directly increase productivity in a pure signaling model. It may work because it separates types.

What Nash equilibrium does and does not say
Nash equilibrium says:

each player is individually optimizing;
each player takes others’ strategies as given;
no player has a profitable unilateral deviation.

It does not say:

the outcome is fair;
the outcome is socially efficient;
the outcome is morally good;
players are happy;
the equilibrium is unique;
players will necessarily learn their way to it.

This distinction is essential.

Nash equilibrium and inefficiency
A Nash equilibrium can be inefficient.
This is one of the main lessons of game theory. Individual rationality does not automatically produce collective welfare.
Examples include:

prisoners’ dilemma;
arms races;
overuse of common resources;
price competition;
public goods underprovision;
failure to coordinate on a better convention.

The general problem is:





flowchart LR
    A[Private incentive] --&gt; B[Individual best response]
    B --&gt; C[Equilibrium]
    C --&gt; D[Possible social inefficiency]

Multiple equilibria
A game may have more than one Nash equilibrium.
Multiple equilibria create a selection problem:

If several outcomes are stable, which one should we expect?

Possible selection forces include:

focal points;
history;
communication;
institutions;
conventions;
risk dominance;
payoff dominance;
learning;
equilibrium refinements.

Coordination games often have multiple equilibria.

Foundations for psychology
Psychology overlaps with these foundations when studying belief formation, bounded reasoning, learning, preference, emotion, and social cognition.
For example:

probability helps describe belief updating;
optimization helps define rational benchmarks;
dynamic reasoning helps analyse planning and self-control;
fixed points help describe stable expectations or norms;
convexity helps formalise trade-offs;
linear algebra helps represent cognitive or social systems.

This is where the site’s broader theme appears: rationality is not only a property of isolated individuals. It is shaped by uncertainty, interaction, time, and institutions.

Matrices
A matrix is a rectangular array of numbers.
For example,
\[
A=
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 2\\
3 & 4
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Matrices can represent:

payoff tables;
systems of equations;
transformations;
networks;
transition probabilities;
data tables.

Current focus
The site is currently being expanded around three priorities:

building useful anchor pages for game theory and foundations;
turning criminology placeholders into mechanism-based notes;
adding diagrams that show logical structure and concept connections.

Stability interpretation
A fixed point is a kind of stability condition. If the system is at the fixed point, applying the rule again does not move it.
In social science, this can describe:

equilibrium strategies;
stable beliefs;
persistent norms;
self-confirming expectations;
steady states;
institutional routines.

The common structure is:
current state → adjustment rule → next state
A fixed point occurs when:
current state = next state

Why dynamic reasoning matters
Timing changes strategic behaviour.
In a simultaneous game, each player chooses without observing the current action of the other. In a dynamic game, one player may observe an earlier move and respond. This creates new strategic possibilities:

threats;
promises;
commitment;
reputation;
punishment;
learning;
signalling;
waiting;
delay.

A dynamic game is not just a static game with time added. The order of moves can change the result.





flowchart LR
    A[Static reasoning] --&gt; B[What is my best response now?]
    C[Dynamic reasoning] --&gt; D[What will happen later?]
    D --&gt; E[What is credible later?]
    E --&gt; F[What should I do now?]






Dynamic reasoning therefore often works backwards: to decide what should happen now, we first ask what would happen later.

Dynamic incentives
A dynamic incentive exists when current behaviour is shaped by future consequences.





flowchart LR
    A[Action today] --&gt; B[Observed by others]
    B --&gt; C[Future response]
    C --&gt; D[Continuation payoff]
    D --&gt; E[Current incentive]






This logic appears in:

repeated games;
reputation models;
relational contracts;
crime and punishment;
trust and betrayal;
cooperation and retaliation;
political promises;
institutional enforcement.

The strategic force is the same: future responses can discipline current behaviour.

What the site is for
The site is meant to support:

learning and reviewing formal concepts;
connecting theory pages with examples;
organizing research notes;
explaining mechanisms visually;
building a bridge between game theory, criminology, and psychology;
keeping track of ideas that may later become essays, teaching materials, or research projects.

Style of the notes
The notes favour:

short definitions;
diagrams where useful;
explicit assumptions;
simple examples before abstract claims;
comparison tables;
links between related concepts;
warnings about common mistakes.

The goal is clarity before completeness.

Definition
A babbling equilibrium is an equilibrium where:

the receiver’s belief after each on-path message is the same as the prior;
the receiver chooses the same action regardless of message;
the sender has no incentive to choose a different message because messages do not affect the receiver’s action.

If the receiver ignores all messages, then the sender is indifferent among messages.

Always exists intuition
In many cheap-talk games, a babbling equilibrium always exists.
The logic is:

receiver ignores messages;
sender messages do not affect payoffs;
sender is willing to send any message;
receiver’s belief that messages are uninformative is confirmed.

This does not mean babbling is the only equilibrium. Informative equilibria may also exist when preferences are sufficiently aligned.

Babbling and real communication
Babbling does not mean people are literally silent or meaningless. It means the message does not change strategic beliefs or actions.
Real examples may include:

empty campaign promises;
routine corporate statements;
excuses known to be self-serving;
warnings from biased sources;
cheap apologies without behavioural consequence;
unverifiable claims from interested parties.

The words may have social meaning, but in the model they transmit no payoff-relevant information.

Repeated games and institutions
Repeated-game logic appears in institutions.
Examples:

firms cooperate because price wars are costly;
communities enforce norms through future exclusion;
criminal groups maintain discipline through repeated interaction;
police and communities build or lose trust over time;
political actors keep promises to protect reputation;
informal contracts survive because future business matters.

Simultaneous does not always mean literal same time
A normal-form game is often called simultaneous-move, but the key idea is informational rather than physical.
Players do not need to choose at exactly the same second. What matters is that a player cannot condition their current action on the other player’s current action.
For example:

two firms set prices without observing each other’s price;
two suspects decide whether to confess;
two countries choose military investment levels;
two students choose how much effort to contribute to a joint project.

Types
A type represents private information about a player.
A type can describe:

cost;
ability;
valuation;
patience;
honesty;
bias;
preference;
information;
intention;
risk;
productivity.

For example, a firm may be high-cost or low-cost:
\[
\theta_i \in \{H,L\}.
\]
The player’s payoff may depend on their own type and possibly on others’ types.

Bayesian games and communication
Once types are private, communication becomes strategic.
An informed player may try to reveal, hide, exaggerate, or manipulate information.
This leads to:

cheap talk;
signaling;
screening;
mechanism design;
reputation;
persuasion;
deception.






flowchart LR
    A[Private type] --&gt; B[Message or action]
    B --&gt; C[Belief update]
    C --&gt; D[Receiver response]
    D --&gt; E[Sender payoff]

Choosing the denominator
A rate depends on the denominator.
Possible denominators include:

population;
households;
firms;
users;
opportunities;
time exposed;
number of transactions;
number of police stops;
number of cases;
number of people at risk.

Different denominators answer different questions.
For example, burglary per resident and burglary per household are not the same measure.

Latent concepts
Some concepts are not directly observed. These are latent concepts.
Examples:

trust;
fear;
self-control;
legitimacy;
belief;
intention;
risk perception;
social cohesion.

Latent concepts require careful measurement. A single proxy may capture only one dimension.





flowchart LR
    A[Latent concept] --&gt; B[Survey item]
    A --&gt; C[Observed behaviour]
    A --&gt; D[Administrative record]
    B --&gt; E[Inference]
    C --&gt; E
    D --&gt; E






Using several measures can improve interpretation.

Belief accuracy
In models of rationality and strategic interaction, we may ask whether beliefs are accurate.
Suppose the true probability of an event is \(p\), while a person believes it is \(\hat p\).
A simple error measure is:
\[
|\hat p - p|.
\]
Belief accuracy matters in:

Bayesian updating;
deterrence;
risk perception;
trust;
reputation;
learning;
prediction.

A person may behave “rationally” given their belief, even if the belief itself is inaccurate.

Welfare comparison
Welfare comparisons ask whether one outcome is socially better than another.
A simple utilitarian welfare function is:
\[
W = \sum_i u_i.
\]
But welfare comparison can be difficult because:

utility may not be comparable across people;
distribution may matter;
rights and fairness may matter;
harm may be multidimensional;
some costs are hidden or delayed.

A model may identify an efficient outcome, but policy interpretation still requires ethical judgement.

Public health approach
A public health approach focuses on reducing harm, not only reducing supply.
Possible mechanisms include:

treatment access;
harm reduction;
overdose prevention;
safer consumption information;
reduced stigma;
housing and support;
substitution treatment;
early intervention.

This approach changes the outcome of interest from “number of arrests” to harm, survival, treatment, and stability.

Descriptive questions
Descriptive questions ask:

What is happening?

Examples:

How has recorded burglary changed over time?
Which areas have high rates of violent crime?
Which groups report lower trust in police?
How do case outcomes vary across courts?

Useful methods include:

summary statistics;
rates and ratios;
time-series plots;
maps;
cross-tabulations;
administrative data summaries;
victimization survey summaries.

Description is not weak. Good description is often the foundation for good explanation.

Causal questions
Causal questions ask:

What is the effect of X on Y?

Examples:

Does hotspot policing reduce crime?
Does incarceration reduce future offending?
Does CCTV reduce theft?
Does procedural justice training improve trust?
Does a sentencing reform change prison populations?

Useful methods include:

randomized experiments;
natural experiments;
difference-in-differences;
regression discontinuity;
instrumental variables;
matching;
panel models.

Causal designs need a credible comparison. The key problem is usually the counterfactual:

What would have happened without the treatment?

Prediction questions
Prediction questions ask:

Can we forecast risk or classify outcomes?

Examples:

Which locations are at higher risk of burglary?
Which cases are likely to drop out?
Which offenders are likely to reoffend?
Which online accounts are likely to be fraudulent?

Useful methods include:

logistic regression;
decision trees;
random forests;
gradient boosting;
survival models;
validation and calibration checks;
error analysis.

Prediction is not the same as causal explanation. A variable may predict an outcome without causing it.

Interpretive questions
Interpretive questions ask:

What meaning do actors attach to behaviour, institutions, or experiences?

Examples:

How do young people understand police stops?
How do victims interpret justice?
How do offenders justify rule-breaking?
How do practitioners understand discretion?

Useful methods include:

interviews;
focus groups;
ethnography;
discourse analysis;
document analysis;
thematic coding.

Interpretive work is especially useful when the research question concerns meaning, identity, legitimacy, fear, stigma, or experience.

Evaluation questions
Evaluation questions ask:

Did a programme or policy work, how did it work, and at what cost?

Examples:

Did an intervention reduce reoffending?
Was the programme implemented as intended?
Did the policy create displacement?
Did the benefits justify the cost?
Did the intervention affect groups unequally?

Useful methods include:

process evaluation;
outcome evaluation;
impact evaluation;
cost-benefit analysis;
implementation analysis;
mixed-method evaluation.

A strong evaluation separates:

whether the programme was implemented;
whether outcomes changed;
whether the programme caused the change;
whether the change was worth the cost.
